======================================================================================================================
L59 -- second-order covariant scalars: does a NONLOCAL quadratic-curvature invariant separate
       the two potentials AND see the coherent field?  (the computation L39/PAPER9 name as open)
======================================================================================================================
  a0 footings: canonical 9.3619e-11 / alt 1.1279e-10 m s^-2
  Milky Way at R_sun = 8.2 kpc, v = 229 km/s  ->  g_gal = 2.0725e-10 m/s^2 = 2.21 a0 (canon) / 1.84 a0 (alt)

======================================================================================================================
A -- CONTROLS.  If any of these fails, the lane stops and says so.
======================================================================================================================

  A1.  L39's operator lemma.  A covariant scalar functional of the metric alone whose expansion begins
  at FIRST order in h has S^(1) = int O^{mn} h_mn with d_m O^{mn} = 0.  Count the transverse symmetric
  O^{mn}(k) available from the background structures.  L39 solved this symbolically; done here instead
  as a NUMERICAL RANK COMPUTATION at random k, which is an independent method.

      basis {eta, k k}            (NO preferred vector): transverse solutions = 1-parameter family
      basis {eta, k k, u u, u k}  (one unit timelike u): transverse solutions = 2-parameter family
  [PASS] A1 [control] exactly ONE transverse symmetric operator with no preferred vector, TWO with one   (reproduces L39 C-b2/C-b3 and L51 A5 by an independent (numerical rank) method)

  A2.  The static two-potential metric, from the EXACT nonlinear metric in sympy.  Convention (L51's):
        ds^2 = -(1 + 2 Phi) dt^2 + (1 - 2 Psi) delta_ij dx^i dx^j
  Phi is the dynamical (Newtonian) potential, Psi the spatial-curvature potential, lensing (Phi+Psi)/2,
  slip s = Phi - Psi, gamma_PPN = Psi/Phi.

  [PASS] A2a [control] linearised R_00 = lap(Phi) for the static two-potential metric   (L51 A3)
  [PASS] A2b [control] linearised R^(1) = 2 lap(2 Psi - Phi)   (R^(1) = -2*Derivative(Phi(x, y, z), (x, 2)) - 2*Derivative(Phi(x, y, z), (y, 2)) - 2*Derivative(Phi(x, y, z), (z, 2)) + 4*Derivative(Psi(x, y, z), (x, 2)) + 4*Derivative(Psi(x, y, z), (y, 2)) + 4*Derivative(Psi(x, y, z), (z, 2)))
  [PASS] A2c [control] linearised G_00 = 2 lap(Psi)   (L51 A3; so 2 lap Psi = 8 pi G rho -> lap Psi = 4 pi G rho)

      S1 = R^(1)            = -2*Derivative(Phi(x, y, z), (x, 2)) - 2*Derivative(Phi(x, y, z), (y, 2)) - 2*Derivative(Phi(x, y, z), (z, 2)) + 4*Derivative(Psi(x, y, z), (x, 2)) + 4*Derivative(Psi(x, y, z), (y, 2)) + 4*Derivative(Psi(x, y, z), (z, 2))
      S2 = R^(1)_mn u^m u^n = Derivative(Phi(x, y, z), (x, 2)) + Derivative(Phi(x, y, z), (y, 2)) + Derivative(Phi(x, y, z), (z, 2))
      map (Phi,Psi) -> (S1,S2) = [[-2, 4], [1, 0]],  determinant = -4
  [PASS] A3 [control] L51's exhibited pair and its determinant -4   (S2 = lap(Phi) isolates the Newtonian potential; (S1+2S2)/4 = lap(Psi) isolates the lensing one; neither is reachable without u)
  [PASS] A3b [control] L39's lock ratio 1:2 -- R^(1) carries ONE fixed combination, both coefficients nonzero   (in L39's own sign convention (Psi <-> Phi, opposite spatial sign) this is the ratio 1 : 2)

  A4.  THE LOCKED IDENTITY, re-derived from an action rather than quoted.  Expand sqrt(-g) R to SECOND
  order in the static two-potential metric, drop total derivatives, and vary with respect to Phi and Psi.
  This is the machine the whole lane runs on, so it is validated on general relativity first.

      Euler-Lagrange of the O(h^2) Einstein-Hilbert Lagrangian:
        dL2/dPhi = 4 lap(Psi)   [+ 0 lap(Phi)]
        dL2/dPsi = -4 lap(Psi) + 4 lap(Phi)
  [PASS] A4a [control] the O(h^2) Einstein-Hilbert Lagrangian varies to  dL/dPhi = k1 lap(Psi) with NO lap(Phi), and dL/dPsi = k2 lap(Psi) + k3 lap(Phi) with k3 = -k2  (so vacuum forces Phi = Psi)   (k1 = 4, k2 = -4, k3 = 4)

      With S = (1/16 pi G) int L2 - int rho Phi + Delta S, and D_Phi = d(Delta S)/dPhi,
      D_Psi = d(Delta S)/dPsi, the two static field equations are

          (4/16 pi G) lap Psi                  = rho - D_Phi
          (-4/16 pi G) lap Psi + (4/16 pi G) lap Phi = - D_Psi

      i.e.    lap Psi      = 4 pi G (rho - D_Phi)
              lap(Phi-Psi) = -4 pi G D_Psi
          =>  lap(Phi+Psi) = 8 pi G rho - 4 pi G ( D_Psi + 2 D_Phi )
              lap Phi      = 4 pi G (rho - D_Phi - D_Psi)

      TWO CRITERIA, used throughout:
        LOCKED IDENTITY  lap(Phi+Psi) = 8 pi G rho          <=>  D_Psi + 2 D_Phi = 0
        CORRECT LENSING  lap(Phi+Psi)/2 = lap Phi           <=>  D_Psi = 0

